                                  FORWARD-LOOKING STATEMENTS

     From time to time, the Guarantor and the Issuer have made and will make forward-looking statements.
These statements can be identified by the fact that they do not relate strictly to historical or current facts.
Forward-looking statements often use words such as “anticipate,” “target,” “expect,” “estimate,” “intend,”
“plan,” “goal,” “believe,” or other words of similar meaning. Forward-looking statements provide the
Guarantor’s and the Issuer’s current expectations or forecasts of future events, circumstances, results or
aspirations. The disclosures in this Base Offering Memorandum, any accompanying Transaction Supplement
and any documents incorporated by reference into this Base Offering Memorandum or any accompanying
Transaction Supplement may contain forward-looking statements. In addition, the senior management of the
Guarantor and the Issuer may make forward-looking statements orally to analysts, investors, representatives
of the media and others.
     All forward-looking statements are, by their nature, subject to risks and uncertainties, many of which are
beyond the Guarantor’s and the Issuer’s control. These risks and uncertainties could cause the Guarantor’s
and the Issuer’s actual future results to differ materially from those set forth in their forward-looking statements.
While there is no assurance that any list of risks and uncertainties is complete, below are certain factors that
could cause actual results to differ from those in the forward-looking statements:
         the Group’s ability to implement the Medium-Term Business Plan and other strategic initiatives and
          measures effectively;
         intensification of competition in the market for financial services in Japan;
         new or increased risks as the Group expands the range of its products and services;
         adverse effects on the Group’s reputation if its business operations are perceived as being harmful
          to the environment and society;
         the effect of climate change;
         MHFG’s failure to establish, maintain and apply adequate internal controls over financial reporting;
         the effect of changes in general economic conditions in Japan and elsewhere;
         increase in allowance for credit losses on loans and incurrence of significant credit-related costs;
         declines in the value of the Guarantor or the Issuer’s securities portfolio, including as a result of the
          declines in stock markets and the impact of the dislocation in the global financial markets;
         changes in interest rates;
         foreign exchange rate fluctuations;
         decrease in the market liquidity of the Guarantor or the Issuer’s assets;
         revised assumptions or other changes related to the Guarantor’s pension plans;
         a decline in the Guarantor’s deferred tax assets;
         the effect of financial transactions entered into for hedging and other similar purposes;
         failure to maintain required capital adequacy ratio levels;
         downgrades in the Guarantor’s credit ratings;
         ability to avoid reputational harm;
         the effectiveness of the Guarantor’s operation, legal and other risk management policies;
         problems related to the Guarantor’s information technology systems and cyber-attacks;
